1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,080
Good morning everyone. In today's seminar, Grant Freeman,

2
00:00:03,080 --> 00:00:07,120
a biologist who specialises in identifying insects

3
00:00:07,120 --> 00:00:08,855
and who works for the Australian Quarantine Service,

4
00:00:08,855 --> 00:00:13,060
has come to talk to us about his current research work

5
00:00:13,320 --> 00:00:15,400
Right, well, over to you, Grant.

6
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:22,750
Good morning, everyone. I'm sure that you know that the quarantine service regulates all food brought into Australia.

7
00:00:22,750 --> 00:00:28,506
Well, obviously they want to protect Australia from diseases that might come in with imported goods,

8
00:00:28,506 --> 00:00:33,542
but they also want to prevent insect pests from being introduced into the country,

9
00:00:33,542 --> 00:00:36,780
and that's where I have a part to play.

10
00:00:36,780 --> 00:00:41,952
Anyway, my current research involves trying to find a particular type of bee,

11
00:00:41,952 --> 00:00:45,940
the Asian Honey Bee, and finding out

12
00:00:45,940 --> 00:00:49,510
whether there are any of them around in various states of Australia.

13
00:00:49,510 --> 00:00:54,250
We discovered a few of them in Queensland once and eradicated them.

14
00:00:54,250 --> 00:00:59,055
Now, we're pretty keen to make sure that there aren't any more getting in,

15
00:00:59,055 --> 00:01:01,800
particularly to New South Wales and other states.

16
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:06,950
What's wrong with Asian Honey Bees? Are they so different from Australian bees?

17
00:01:06,950 --> 00:01:10,360
Well, in fact, they look almost the same

18
00:01:10,620 --> 00:01:13,565
but they are infested with mites- microscopic creatures

19
00:01:13,565 --> 00:01:19,640
which live on them, and which can seriously damage our own home-grown bees

20
00:01:19,640 --> 00:01:21,420
or could even wipe them out.

21
00:01:21,420 --> 00:01:24,790
Well, what would happen if Australian bees died out?

22
00:01:24,790 --> 00:01:28,601
Well, the honey from Australian bees is of excellent quality,

23
00:01:28,601 --> 00:01:32,030
much better than the stuff the Asian bees produce.

24
00:01:32,030 --> 00:01:38,050
In fact, Australia exports native Queen bees to a large number of countries because of this.

25
00:01:38,050 --> 00:01:42,520
When the European Honey Bee was first discovered out in the bush

26
00:01:42,960 --> 00:01:44,890
we found they made really unpleasant honey

27
00:01:44,890 --> 00:01:50,650
and they were also too big to pollinate many of our native flowers here in Australia.

28
00:01:50,650 --> 00:01:54,400
That must have had a devastating effect on the natural flora

29
00:01:55,040 --> 00:01:56,780
Did you lose any species?

30
00:01:56,780 --> 00:02:00,720
No, we managed to get them under control before that happened,

31
00:02:00,720 --> 00:02:04,660
but if Asian bees got in there could be other consequences.

32
00:02:04,660 --> 00:02:09,395
We could lose a lot of money because you might not be aware,

33
00:02:09,395 --> 00:02:12,673
but it's estimated that native bees' pollination of flower

34
00:02:12,673 --> 00:02:16,680
and vegetable crops is worth 1.2 billion dollars a year.

35
00:02:16,680 --> 00:02:23,516
So in a way they're the farmers' friend. Oh, and another thing is, if you're stung by an Asian Honey Bee,

36
00:02:23,516 --> 00:02:26,400
it can produce an allergic reaction in some people

37
00:02:26,440 --> 00:02:29,050
so they're much more dangerous than native bees.

38
00:02:29,050 --> 00:02:32,750
How will you know if Asian bees have entered Australia?

39
00:02:32,750 --> 00:02:37,220
We're looking at the diet of the bird called the Rainbow Bee Eater.

40
00:02:37,220 --> 00:02:41,660
The Bee Eater doesn't care what it eats, as long as they're insects.

41
00:02:41,660 --> 00:02:47,606
But the interesting thing about this bird is that we are able to analyse exactly what it eats

42
00:02:47,606 --> 00:02:51,570
and that's really helpful if we're looking for introduced insects. How come?

43
00:02:51,570 --> 00:02:55,320
Because insects have their skeletons outside their bodies

44
00:02:55,520 --> 00:02:58,750
so the Bee Eaters digest the meat from the inside.

45
00:02:58,750 --> 00:03:02,808
Then they bring up all the indigestible bits of skeleton and,

46
00:03:02,808 --> 00:03:05,360
of course, the wings in a pellet

47
00:03:05,560 --> 00:03:09,080
a small ball of waste material which they cough up.

48
00:03:09,080 --> 00:03:12,470
That sounds a bit unpleasant. So, how do you go about it?

49
00:03:12,470 --> 00:03:17,195
In the field we track down the Bee Eaters and find their favourite feeding spots,

50
00:03:17,195 --> 00:03:20,030
you know, the places where the birds usually feed.

51
00:03:20,030 --> 00:03:22,740
It's here that we can find the pellets.

52
00:03:22,740 --> 00:03:27,820
We collect them up and take them back to the laboratory to examine the contents.

53
00:03:27,820 --> 00:03:31,440
How do you do that? The pellets are really hard

54
00:03:31,520 --> 00:03:35,396
especially if they have been out in the sun for a few days

55
00:03:35,396 --> 00:03:40,995
so first of all, we treat them by adding water to moisten them and make them softer.

56
00:03:40,995 --> 00:03:43,630
Then we pull them apart under the microscope.

57
00:03:43,630 --> 00:03:47,780
Everything's all scrunched up but we're looking for wings

58
00:03:47,780 --> 00:03:50,530
so we just pull them all out and straighten them.

59
00:03:50,530 --> 00:03:54,680
Then we identify them to see if we can find any Asian bee wings

60
00:03:55,180 --> 00:03:56,460
And how many have you found?

61
00:03:56,460 --> 00:04:01,728
So far our research shows that Asian bees have not entered Australia in any number -

62
00:04:01,728 --> 00:04:08,400
it's a good result and much more reliable than trying to find live ones as evidence of introduced insects.

63
00:04:08,400 --> 00:04:12,200
Well, that's fascinating! Thank you, Grant, for those insights.

64
00:04:12,200 --> 00:04:17,340
I hope that you might inspire some of our students here to conduct some similar experiments.
